CAT = "sirMinors"; SEG = "MIN"
Q = [
("Under BC's Act, which of these is an offence when the drinker is a minor?",
 'Letting them drink at home', ['Serving them a soft drink', 'Seating them for a meal', 'Asking them for photo ID'],
 'Section 77 says a person must not allow a minor to consume or possess liquor in a place under their control, as well as not supplying liquor to one.'),
("Buying liquor now to give to a 17-year-old cousin later is:",
 "An offence under the Act", ["Legal if it stays sealed", "Legal with a parent's note", "Only a store matter"],
 "Section 77(1)(b) bars possessing liquor for the purpose of supplying it to a minor, even before it is handed over."),
("How many pieces of identification make up prescribed ID in BC?",
 "Two", ["One", "Two", "Three", "Four"],
 "Regulation s. 158 prescribes two pieces: one primary photo ID with the date of birth and one more showing the person's name and a signature or photo.", "fixed"),
("Which of these is a primary piece of ID under BC's Regulation?",
 "Passport", ["Credit card", "Student card", "Library card"],
 "Regulation s. 158 lists a passport, a photo driver's licence showing the date of birth, or a government-issued photo ID card showing the date of birth as primary ID."),
("A photo driver's licence counts as primary ID only if it shows:",
 'Date of birth', ['Home address', 'Blood type', 'Licence class'],
 "Regulation s. 158 accepts a photo driver's licence as primary ID when it shows the person's date of birth."),
("The second piece of ID must show the person's name and:",
 'Signature or photo', ['Date of birth', 'Home address', 'Expiry date'],
 'Regulation s. 158 requires the second piece to show the name and either a signature, a photo or both. The date of birth comes from the primary ID.'),
("Which pair meets BC's prescribed ID rule?",
 "Passport and signed bank card", ["Two credit cards", "Birth certificate and bus pass", "Student card and library card"],
 "A passport is primary photo ID with the date of birth, and a bank card with the name and a signature is a valid second piece. The other pairs have no primary photo ID."),
('What protects a server who reasonably concluded a minor was 19?',
 'Checking prescribed ID', ['Seeing them arrive with adults', "Writing the guest's name down", 'Asking their age out loud'],
 'Sections 77(3) and 79(3) give a defence when the person required prescribed identification, examined it and had reason to believe it was authentic.'),
('For the ID defence to work, the server must:',
 'Examine it and believe it', ['Photocopy it and keep it', 'Phone the issuing office', 'Have a manager sign it'],
 'The defence in s. 77(3) needs the server to require prescribed ID, examine it and have reason to believe it is authentic. A glance without checking is not enough.'),
("Using an older sibling's driver's licence to get into a bar is:",
 "An offence by the minor", ["Legal if they look alike", "Legal with the sibling there", "Only the bar's problem"],
 "Section 78(3) says a minor must not present identification issued to another person, or altered, forged or fraudulently made ID, to buy liquor or enter a service area."),
('A minor who drinks a beer a friend bought them in a bar has broken the law by:',
 'Drinking it', ['Nothing; the friend did', 'Only entering the bar', 'Nothing; it was a gift'],
 'Section 78(1) says a minor must not buy, consume or possess liquor except as the Act allows. The friend who supplied it also commits an offence under s. 77.'),
('A 17-year-old orders a soda at a bar and is helping a police compliance check. Under the Act:',
 'Allowed while doing that job', ['Must be arrested at once', 'The bar must close for the night', 'May drink to test the staff'],
 'Section 78(4) exempts a minor working with police or the general manager to test licensee compliance, while supervised. It never permits the minor to drink.'),
('At a food primary restaurant, a 16-year-old server may:',
 'Serve drinks', ['Mix cocktails', 'Open wine bottles', 'Pour draught beer'],
 'Regulation s. 164 lets minors 16 or older sell or serve liquor in a food primary establishment, but not mix drinks or open liquor containers.'),
('What is the youngest age at which a minor may serve liquor in a food primary?',
 '16', ['16', '17', '18', '19'],
 'Regulation s. 164 allows minors aged 16 or older to sell or serve liquor in a food primary, without mixing drinks or opening containers.',
 'fixed'),
('A minor working where liquor is served must be:',
 'Continually supervised', ['Paid above minimum wage', 'Off the floor after 9 p.m.', 'Paired with another minor'],
 'Regulation s. 162 requires direct and continual supervision by the licensee, permittee or an adult employee whenever minors are employed.'),
('In a liquor primary nightclub, a 17-year-old employee may:',
 'Non-liquor jobs only', ['Serve beer but not spirits', 'Serve once they are trained', 'Pour drinks behind the bar'],
 'Regulation s. 163 limits minors in a liquor primary (other than a stadium) to jobs that do not involve liquor, and only when minors are allowed in that area.'),
('At a stadium, a 16-year-old staff member may not:',
 'Hawk drinks in the stands', ['Serve a drink at a counter', 'Sell bottled soft drinks', 'Clear empty cups'],
 'Regulation s. 163(2) lets stadium minors 16 or older sell or serve liquor, but not mix drinks, open containers or hawk liquor.'),
('A liquor primary establishment must not be:',
 'Aimed at minors', ['Open past midnight', 'Next to a restaurant', 'Run by one family'],
 'Regulation s. 10 says a liquor primary establishment cannot be directed at, or frequented mainly by, minors.'),
('Who commits an offence when a licensee lets a minor stay where the licence bars minors?',
 'Licensee and staff', ['Only the minor', "Only the minor's parents", 'No one if nothing is sold'],
 'Section 79(1) says a licensee, permittee, employee or liquor seller must not permit a minor to enter or remain where the licence terms or regulations prohibit it.'),
("On November 23, 2026, ID shows a birth date of November 24, 2007. The guest is:",
 "18, a minor", ["19, an adult", "20, an adult", "17, a minor"],
 "Born November 24, 2007, the guest turns 19 on November 24, 2026, one day later. On November 23, 2026 they are still 18 and a minor in BC."),
("On November 23, 2026, which birth date belongs to someone who is already 19?",
 "November 23, 2007", ["November 24, 2007", "December 1, 2007", "January 5, 2008"],
 "A person born November 23, 2007 turns 19 on November 23, 2026. The later dates are all still 18 on that day."),
("A guest born June 30, 2008 can first be served liquor in BC on:",
 "June 30, 2027", ["June 30, 2026", "June 29, 2027", "July 1, 2027"],
 "The age of majority is 19, reached on the 19th birthday: June 30, 2008 plus 19 years is June 30, 2027."),
("The photo on a licence looks like the guest, but the laminate is peeling around it. Staff should:",
 "Treat it as possibly altered", ["Accept it; the face matches", "Peel it back to check", "Accept it with a second card"],
 "The ID defence needs reason to believe the ID is authentic, and altered ID is an offence under s. 78(3). Signs of tampering mean staff should not rely on it."),
("Why is a birth certificate not primary ID under BC's Regulation?",
 "It has no photo", ["It has no signature", "It is too old", "It is issued federally"],
 "Every primary ID in Regulation s. 158 is photo ID with the date of birth: a passport, a photo driver's licence or a government photo ID card. A birth certificate has no photo."),
('A guest says they forgot their ID but their friends vouch they are 22. The server should:',
 'Decline to serve them', ['Serve them one drink', 'Serve if friends show ID', 'Serve after an age quiz'],
 "Without prescribed identification there is no defence if the guest turns out to be a minor. A friend's word is not ID."),
('A parent orders two beers at a pub and passes one to their 17-year-old. The server should:',
 'Stop it; it is unlawful', ['Allow it, a parent agreed', 'Allow it with a meal', 'Allow it at a booth'],
 "Section 77 bars anyone from supplying liquor to a minor, and the licensee must not let a minor consume liquor in the establishment. A parent's consent does not make it lawful there."),
('A group of adults keeps ordering extra drinks after a teen joins their table. Staff should:',
 'Watch for passed drinks', ['Cut off the whole group', 'Move the teen to another room', 'Charge the teen a cover fee'],
 'Licensees must not let a minor consume or possess liquor on the premises (s. 77(1)(c)). Watching for adults passing drinks to minors is part of supervising the room.'),
("An inspector asks a young-looking patron for ID during an inspection. This is:",
 "Allowed under the Act", ["Allowed only with a warrant", "Allowed only for staff", "Not allowed for patrons"],
 "Section 42 lets the general manager's inspectors require patrons, employees and contractors of a licensee to produce identification."),
("A minor in a bar where minors are not allowed may be committing an offence because:",
 "Minors must not be there", ["They brought a coat", "They paid a cover charge", "They used the washroom"],
 "Section 78(2) bars a minor from entering or being in a service area except as the licence terms, regulations or other lawful excuse allow."),
('At a restaurant, a 16-year-old performs in a band. Minors may entertain only:',
 'As the licence allows', ['After 10 p.m. only', 'With no adult present', 'When paid in cash'],
 'Regulation s. 166 lets minors entertain patrons only in listed kinds of venues where the licence or permit allows it, and never in adult entertainment.'),
('Who must prove the defence that ID was asked for and examined?',
 'Person charged', ['The minor', 'The police', "The minor's parent"],
 'Sections 77(3) and 79(3) set out a defence available to the person charged: that they required prescribed ID, examined it and reasonably believed it authentic. A habit of checking ID is what makes it available.'),
('Selling liquor to a 19-year-old who then gives it to a 16-year-old makes the giver:',
 'Guilty of supplying', ['Not responsible at all', 'Guilty only if paid', 'Guilty only in a bar'],
 'Section 77(1)(a) bars any person from selling, giving or otherwise supplying liquor to a minor. It applies anywhere, not only in licensed places.'),
]
